# The devtools point at this page

Loom Devtools drops a bar over a running application so a reviewer can leave a note by pointing at what they mean instead of describing it. The landing makes that claim in one section, and the section proves it rather than picturing it: the page a reader is on is the application, and the tools work.

Arming the picker turns the whole page into a target. Whatever sits under the cursor takes a dashed outline labelled with what a note filed there would point at. Clicking it opens the notes panel with that pointer already attached, waiting for a sentence.

One boundary is worth stating plainly. No daemon stands behind a marketing page, so the panel keeps its notes in the model and forgets them when the page reloads. Everything the reviewer does up to that point — aim, pin, write, resolve, reopen, discard, collapse the bar — is the real interaction, not a mime of it.

## What a note points at

A note is worth what the thing it names is worth, so naming the target well is most of the work. The picker names it in three tiers and takes the first that matches.

The best answer is a fragment that a `.loom` section wove. Rendered Loom source carries the chapter and the section that produced it, so the note lands in the document a developer would edit rather than in the markup that document generated.

Below it sits an element the page has already named for itself. A `data-pick` attribute holds the author's own words for that element, so a note reads `button.cta` rather than a selector nobody wrote.

Everything else the picker names the way any picker would: the tag, its identifier when it has one, and its seat among siblings of the same tag. A section identifier scopes the result unless the element carries an identifier of its own, so `loom-devtools p:nth-of-type(2)` says which paragraph in which section.

=>

import { Array, Match, Option, pipe } from 'effect'
import type { Html } from 'foldkit/html'
import {
  ClearedTarget,
  ClosedNotes,
  CollapsedBar,
  DiscardedNote,
  DraftedNote,
  ExpandedBar,
  type Highlight,
  type Message,
  type Model,
  type Note,
  type NoteTab,
  ResolvedNote,
  type Route,
  SentNote,
  ShowedNotes,
  type Target,
  ToggledNotes,
  ToggledPicker,
  h,
} from './model'
import {
  broadSeam,
  carpetMark,
  devtoolsCarpet,
  downIcon,
  noteIcon,
  pickerIcon,
  upIcon,
} from './components'

const WOVEN = '[data-loom-chapter][data-loom-section]'

const wovenTarget = (element: Element): Option.Option<Target> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(element.closest(WOVEN)),
    Option.flatMap((woven) =>
      Option.all([
        Option.fromNullishOr(woven.getAttribute('data-loom-chapter')),
        Option.fromNullishOr(woven.getAttribute('data-loom-section')),
      ]),
    ),
    Option.map(([chapter, section]) => ({
      kind: 'loom' as const,
      label: section,
      pointer: `${chapter} / ${section}`,
    })),
  )

const namedTarget = (element: Element): Option.Option<Target> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(element.closest('[data-pick]')),
    Option.flatMap((named) => Option.fromNullishOr(named.getAttribute('data-pick'))),
    Option.map((name) => ({ kind: 'dom' as const, label: name, pointer: name })),
  )

~

The third tier is the one that has to be built rather than read. The seat is a `:nth-of-type` suffix, added only when the element has siblings of its own tag, since a lone paragraph needs no ordinal. The scope is the enclosing section's identifier, which the landing and the argument page both carry.

=>

const SECTIONS = '[id^="loom-"], [id^="why-"]'

const kinOf = (element: Element): ReadonlyArray<Element> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(element.parentElement),
    Option.map((parent) =>
      Array.filter(
        Array.fromIterable(parent.children),
        (child) => child.tagName === element.tagName,
      ),
    ),
    Option.getOrElse((): ReadonlyArray<Element> => [element]),
  )

const seatOf = (element: Element): string => {
  const kin = kinOf(element)
  return kin.length < 2
    ? ''
    : pipe(
        Array.findFirstIndex(kin, (child) => child === element),
        Option.map((index) => `:nth-of-type(${index + 1})`),
        Option.getOrElse(() => ''),
      )
}

const scopeOf = (element: Element): string =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(element.closest(SECTIONS)),
    Option.map((section) => `${section.id} `),
    Option.getOrElse(() => ''),
  )

const selectorTarget = (element: Element): Target => {
  const name = element.tagName.toLowerCase()
  const identifier = element.id === '' ? '' : `#${element.id}`
  const label = `${name}${identifier}${seatOf(element)}`
  return {
    kind: 'dom',
    label,
    pointer: identifier === '' ? `${scopeOf(element)}${label}` : label,
  }
}

export const targetOf = (element: Element): Target =>
  pipe(
    wovenTarget(element),
    Option.orElse(() => namedTarget(element)),
    Option.getOrElse(() => selectorTarget(element)),
  )

~

Every note also records where the reviewer was standing, since the same element can appear on more than one page and a pointer alone would not say which. The path is matched off the route rather than read from the browser, so the stamp is the same whether the page arrived from the server or from a link the client followed.

=>

export const pathOf = (route: Route): string =>
  Match.value(route).pipe(
    Match.when('home', () => '/'),
    Match.when('docs', () => '/docs'),
    Match.when('why-loom', () => '/why-loom'),
    Match.when('community', () => '/community'),
    Match.when('source', () => '/source'),
    Match.exhaustive,
  )

## What the picker may aim at

The picker reaches the whole page except itself. Aiming at the bar or the panel would let a reviewer file a note about the tool they are filing it with, which is noise, so anything inside the devtools chrome is refused. So are the document and the body, which are the page rather than anything on it.

The outline is measured where a fixed element is drawn, in coordinates the browser reckons from the top-left of the window, and grown three pixels on every side so it sits around what it marks rather than on top of it.

=>

const CHROME = '[data-dt-chrome]'

export const aimableAt = (node: EventTarget | null): Option.Option<Element> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(node),
    Option.filter((found): found is Element => found instanceof Element),
    Option.filter((element) => element.closest(CHROME) === null),
    Option.filter(
      (element) => element !== document.body && element !== document.documentElement,
    ),
  )

export const highlightOf = (element: Element): Highlight => {
  const box = element.getBoundingClientRect()
  return {
    label: targetOf(element).label,
    left: box.left - 3,
    top: box.top - 3,
    width: box.width + 6,
    height: box.height + 6,
  }
}

## The notes it opens with

A panel that opens empty teaches nothing, so the section starts with three notes already filed — one of each kind, so a reader sees at once what the three colours mean. Two are open and one is resolved, which is what puts a number on the bar and gives the resolved tab something to show.

They are seeded rather than fetched. Both the server and the browser build their first model from this list, so the count on the bar is the same before and after the page hydrates and nothing jumps.

=>

export const seedNotes: ReadonlyArray<Note> = [
  {
    seq: 1,
    kind: 'chat',
    pointer: '',
    route: '/',
    text: 'Say plainly that the bar never ships to production.',
    addressed: false,
  },
  {
    seq: 2,
    kind: 'dom',
    pointer: 'p.what',
    route: '/',
    text: 'Call it an anchor here, not an identifier.',
    addressed: false,
  },
  {
    seq: 3,
    kind: 'loom',
    pointer: '10-the-devtools / The two columns',
    route: '/',
    text: 'Cut this paragraph to two sentences.',
    addressed: true,
  },
]

## The bar, and the handle it leaves

The bar names the project it is watching and carries three controls: the picker, the notes with a count of the open ones, and a chevron that folds it away. Collapsing it leaves a handle at the right edge, still showing the count, because a reviewer who has hidden the bar has not stopped caring how many notes are waiting.

Both the picker button and the notes button light their ground when their tool is active, which is the only signal that the picker is armed once the reviewer's eye has moved to the page.

=>

const bar = (model: Model): Html =>
  h.div(
    [h.Class('dt-bar')],
    [
      h.span([h.Class('dt-project')], ['loom-website']),
      h.span(
        [h.Class('dt-tools')],
        [
          h.button(
            [
              h.Class(model.picking ? 'dt-tool picking' : 'dt-tool'),
              h.Type('button'),
              h.Title('Pick an element'),
              h.OnClick(ToggledPicker()),
            ],
            [pickerIcon()],
          ),
          h.button(
            [
              h.Class(panelOpen(model) ? 'dt-tool showing' : 'dt-tool'),
              h.Type('button'),
              h.Title(model.notesOpen ? 'Hide notes' : `Notes (${openNotes(model).length})`),
              h.OnClick(ToggledNotes()),
            ],
            [noteIcon(), h.span([h.Class('dt-count')], [String(openNotes(model).length)])],
          ),
          h.button(
            [
              h.Class('dt-tool'),
              h.Type('button'),
              h.Title('Hide the bar'),
              h.OnClick(CollapsedBar()),
            ],
            [downIcon()],
          ),
        ],
      ),
    ],
  )

const handle = (model: Model): Html =>
  h.div(
    [h.Class('dt-handle-row')],
    [
      h.button(
        [
          h.Class('dt-handle'),
          h.Type('button'),
          h.Title('Show the bar'),
          h.OnClick(ExpandedBar()),
        ],
        [upIcon(), h.span([h.Class('dt-count')], [String(openNotes(model).length)])],
      ),
    ],
  )

~

Two questions get asked from several places, so they are answered once. The open notes are the ones nobody has addressed. The panel shows only when it has been opened, the bar has not been collapsed under it, and the picker is not armed — a reviewer taking aim wants the page, not a panel over it.

=>

const openNotes = (model: Model): ReadonlyArray<Note> =>
  Array.filter(model.notes, (note) => !note.addressed)

const panelOpen = (model: Model): boolean =>
  model.notesOpen && !model.barCollapsed && !model.picking

## The two columns

The left column is what a reader reads: the paragraph that says what the three kinds of note are. The right column is the working half — the bar, and a fragment stamped as woven Loom for the picker to aim at. Nothing else stands in that column, since the section asks a reader to try one thing and a way onward would only invite them to leave before they had. Reading and aiming are two different things to be doing, so the section gives each its own side rather than mixing them down both. The two columns hold until nine hundred pixels, where they become a row a reader swipes, like the marks and the commands above them. Reading and aiming stay side by side as long as the window allows it, because a devtools bar squeezed into a phone's width is a bar nobody can aim with.

The stamped fragment is not a mock. It carries the same two attributes a real weave writes, and the picker reads them by the same rule it uses on the reading website, which is why aiming at it produces a chapter and a section rather than a `div`.

=>

const kinds = (): Html =>
  h.p(
    [h.Class('dt-what'), h.DataAttribute('pick', 'p.what')],
    [
      'Devtools unite complementary tools for a literate programming experience. Point at anything and leave a note on it. A ',
      h.span([h.Class('dt-kind dom')], ['dom']),
      " note carries the element's selector and the label you clicked; a ",
      h.span([h.Class('dt-kind chat')], ['chat']),
      ' note carries the route you were on; a ',
      h.span([h.Class('dt-kind loom')], ['loom']),
      ' note on rendered Loom source resolves back to the exact place in the ',
      h.span([h.Class('dt-kind file')], ['.loom']),
      ' file that produced it.',
    ],
  )

const wovenSample = (): Html =>
  h.div(
    [
      h.Class('dt-woven'),
      h.DataAttribute('loom-chapter', '02-pricing'),
      h.DataAttribute('loom-section', 'Rounding money'),
    ],
    [
      h.p([h.Class('dt-woven-says'), h.DataAttribute('pick', 'p')], [
        'Pick any element here to see notes get the loom tier.',
      ]),
      h.div([h.Class('dt-woven-code'), h.DataAttribute('pick', 'div')], [
        h.span([h.Class('tok-keyword')], ['const']),
        ' foo = (n: number): string => "bar"',
      ]),
    ],
  )

## The notes panel

The panel floats at the bottom-right of the window rather than inside the section, because devtools belongs to the application and not to the page it is watching. It holds a header with the two tabs, the list, and the composer.

A row states the note's number, its kind in that kind's colour, its pointer, and the route it was filed on, then the sentence, then the two things that can be done to it. The action reads `resolve` on an open note and `reopen` on an addressed one, since the same button undoes what it did.

=>

const noteRow = (note: Note): Html =>
  h.div(
    [h.Class('dt-note')],
    [
      h.div(
        [h.Class('dt-note-head')],
        [
          h.span([h.Class('dt-note-seq')], [`#${note.seq}`]),
          h.span([h.Class(`dt-kind ${note.kind}`)], [note.kind]),
          h.span([h.Class('dt-note-pointer')], [note.pointer]),
          h.span([h.Class('dt-note-route')], [note.route]),
        ],
      ),
      h.span([h.Class('dt-note-text')], [note.text]),
      h.div(
        [h.Class('dt-note-acts')],
        [
          h.button(
            [h.Class('dt-act resolve'), h.Type('button'), h.OnClick(ResolvedNote({ seq: note.seq }))],
            [note.addressed ? 'reopen' : 'resolve'],
          ),
          h.button(
            [h.Class('dt-act'), h.Type('button'), h.OnClick(DiscardedNote({ seq: note.seq }))],
            ['discard'],
          ),
        ],
      ),
    ],
  )

~

The list shows one half of the notes at a time, and says so when its half is empty. The two empty lines differ because the two emptinesses mean different things: nothing resolved yet is a statement of fact, while nothing open is an invitation to leave one.

=>

const shownNotes = (model: Model): ReadonlyArray<Note> =>
  Match.value(model.noteTab).pipe(
    Match.when('open', () => openNotes(model)),
    Match.when('resolved', () => Array.filter(model.notes, (note) => note.addressed)),
    Match.exhaustive,
  )

const emptyLine = (tab: NoteTab): string =>
  Match.value(tab).pipe(
    Match.when('open', () => 'No open notes. Pick something and leave one.'),
    Match.when('resolved', () => 'Nothing resolved yet.'),
    Match.exhaustive,
  )

const noteList = (model: Model): Html => {
  const shown = shownNotes(model)
  return h.div(
    [h.Class('dt-list')],
    Array.isReadonlyArrayEmpty(shown)
      ? [h.div([h.Class('dt-empty')], [emptyLine(model.noteTab)])]
      : Array.map(shown, noteRow),
  )
}

const tab = (model: Model, which: NoteTab, label: string): Html =>
  h.button(
    [
      h.Class(model.noteTab === which ? 'dt-tab here' : 'dt-tab'),
      h.Type('button'),
      h.OnClick(ShowedNotes({ tab: which })),
    ],
    [label],
  )

~

The composer is where a target becomes a note. When the reviewer has aimed at something, a chip above the field names it and offers to drop it; sending without one files a `chat` note about the page instead. The send button goes dark only once there is something to send, so an empty field reads as unfinished rather than broken.

=>

const draftKey = (key: string, model: Model): Message =>
  Match.value(key).pipe(
    Match.when('Enter', () => SentNote()),
    Match.orElse(() => DraftedNote({ text: model.draft })),
  )

const aimedChip = (target: Target): Html =>
  h.div(
    [h.Class('dt-chip')],
    [
      h.span([h.Class('dt-chip-eye')], ['◎']),
      h.span([h.Class('dt-chip-label')], [target.pointer]),
      h.button(
        [h.Class('dt-chip-drop'), h.Type('button'), h.OnClick(ClearedTarget())],
        ['✕'],
      ),
    ],
  )

const composer = (model: Model): Html =>
  h.div(
    [h.Class('dt-composer')],
    [
      ...Option.match(model.aimed, {
        onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<Html> => [],
        onSome: (target) => [aimedChip(target)],
      }),
      h.input([
        h.Class('dt-draft'),
        h.Value(model.draft),
        h.Placeholder('Leave a note…'),
        h.OnInput((text) => DraftedNote({ text })),
        h.OnKeyDown((key) => draftKey(key, model)),
      ]),
      h.div(
        [h.Class('dt-send-row')],
        [
          h.button(
            [
              h.Class(model.picking ? 'dt-pick-chip picking' : 'dt-pick-chip'),
              h.Type('button'),
              h.OnClick(ToggledPicker()),
            ],
            [model.picking ? 'Escape to cancel' : 'Pick element'],
          ),
          h.button(
            [
              h.Class(model.draft.trim() === '' ? 'dt-send' : 'dt-send ready'),
              h.Type('button'),
              h.OnClick(SentNote()),
            ],
            ['Send'],
          ),
        ],
      ),
    ],
  )

const panel = (model: Model): Html =>
  h.div(
    [h.Class('dt-panel'), h.DataAttribute('dt-chrome', '1')],
    [
      h.div(
        [h.Class('dt-panel-head')],
        [
          h.span([h.Class('dt-panel-title')], ['Notes']),
          h.span([h.Class('dt-tabs')], [tab(model, 'open', 'open'), tab(model, 'resolved', 'resolved')]),
          h.button(
            [h.Class('dt-close'), h.Type('button'), h.OnClick(ClosedNotes())],
            ['✕'],
          ),
        ],
      ),
      noteList(model),
      composer(model),
    ],
  )

## The outline the picker paints

The outline is two fixed elements: the dashed rectangle and the label above it. Both are drawn from the highlight the model holds, and both leave the page entirely when it holds none, so nothing lingers in the markup for a screen reader to read out.

=>

const outline = (highlight: Highlight): ReadonlyArray<Html> => [
  h.span(
    [
      h.AriaHidden(true),
      h.Class('dt-outline'),
      h.Style({
        left: `${highlight.left}px`,
        top: `${highlight.top}px`,
        width: `${highlight.width}px`,
        height: `${highlight.height}px`,
      }),
    ],
    [],
  ),
  h.span(
    [
      h.AriaHidden(true),
      h.Class('dt-outline-label'),
      h.Style({ left: `${highlight.left}px`, top: `${highlight.top - 16}px` }),
    ],
    [highlight.label],
  ),
]

## The section

The section head wears the devtools carpet, and the two columns sit under it. The panel and the outline are appended last, so they paint over everything the page has drawn, and each appears only when the model says it should.

=>

export const devtools = (model: Model): Html =>
  h.div(
    [h.Class('section'), h.Id('loom-devtools')],
    [
      h.div(
        [h.Class('section-head')],
        [
          carpetMark(devtoolsCarpet, '42px', broadSeam),
          h.div(
            [h.Class('lines')],
            [
              h.span([h.Class('eyebrow'), h.DataAttribute('pick', 'span.eyebrow')], [
                'Complementary tools',
              ]),
              h.h2([h.DataAttribute('pick', 'h2#devtools')], ['Loom Devtools']),
            ],
          ),
        ],
      ),
      h.div(
        [h.Class('dt-grid')],
        [
          h.div([h.Class('dt-col')], [kinds()]),
          h.div(
            [h.Class('dt-col dt-aim')],
            [
              h.div(
                [h.Class('dt-chrome'), h.DataAttribute('dt-chrome', '1')],
                [model.barCollapsed ? handle(model) : bar(model)],
              ),
              wovenSample(),
            ],
          ),
        ],
      ),
      ...(panelOpen(model) ? [panel(model)] : []),
      ...Option.match(model.highlight, {
        onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<Html> => [],
        onSome: outline,
      }),
    ],
  )

## The module {Tangle}[devtools.ts]

The module is what a note points at, what the picker may aim at, the notes it opens with, the bar and its handle, the two columns, the notes panel, the outline, and the section that holds them.

